 { François-André Danican Philidor, the leading player of his time, is credited with having said that “the pawns are the soul of chess”. This is especially true when speaking of positional play. In this chapter, we’ll cover the most important aspects of pawn play that you must master in order to develop into a strong positional player. These are: (1) Pawn breaks, both as an attacking weapon and studying ways to defend against them. (2) How to target and undermine the opponent's pawn structure, including by launching a 'minority attack'. (3) How to use your pawns to target the opponent's pieces (4) How to use the 4 vs 3 pawn majority to your advantage. (5) Various pawn types like doubled or isolated pawns, and key techniques based around these. We'll begin by discussing one of the most important weapons in any chess player's toolkit: the pawn break! See you there. } 1. { -KEY- } -- 
